WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:03.003
In the previous lesson, you built the functionality

2
00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:06.106
to discover relevant APIs from your knowledge graph

3
00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:08.308
based on their semantic embeddings

4
00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:10.543
and their business process relations.

5
00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:14.647
In this lesson, you will use this to enable an agent

6
00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:19.119
to discover and execute these APIs to fulfill user queries.

7
00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:22.922
Let's go. Here's what you will do in this lesson.

8
00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:26.226
You'll create an agent that executes user queries

9
00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:29.729
for get purchase orders and create purchase order.

10
00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:32.298
You will define three tools for this agent.

11
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:36.803
The discovery tool, the GET Data tool, and the POST Data tool.

12
00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:39.806
The discovery tool retrieves API metadata

13
00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:43.810
such as properties, navigations, and business process information.

14
00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:46.379
The GET Data tool is used

15
00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.081
for get purchase order related queries.

16
00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:50.784
The POST Data tool enables creation

17
00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:54.287
of a purchase requisition
and a purchase order,

18
00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.524
based on business process information from the Knowledge Graph.

19
00:00:57.991 --> 00:01:00.193
Let's now get started with the notebook.

20
00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.196
First, let's import all the necessary packages.

21
00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:06.399
As in the last lessons, we import custom helper functions

22
00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.601
to parameterize SPARQL queries

23
00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.904
and one for fetching OpenAI key.

24
00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.206
Next, we set up LangChain models

25
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.909
for the LLM and the embedding model we're using.

26
00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:20.480
Next, we load the knowledge graph we created in the previous lessons.

27
00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:24.184
Now, let's fetch API specifications

28
00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:27.187
to include properties of entity sets

29
00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:29.856
and navigation links of the entity sets.

30
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.194
For defining the discovery tool, we need to fetch properties

31
00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:36.896
and navigation links of entity sets.

32
00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:40.467
This helper function fetches and combines them both.

33
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.369
Let's now try this with an example query.

34
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.475
Here we fetch entity specification for the purchase order entity set,

35
00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:50.210
and in the results you'll see that

36
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.845
there is a service for purchase order,

37
00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:54.347
the corresponding name of the entity set

38
00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:58.151
along with its properties such as PurchasingProcessingStatus,

39
00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:03.423
PurchasingGroup and so on. You'll also see that purchase order number

40
00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:06.226
is the key for this particular entity set.

41
00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:08.428
And we also retrieve the navigations

42
00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:11.731
for this particular entity set, which is related

43
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:15.735
to PurchaseOrderItem with a cardinality of one_to_many.

44
00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:18.238
implying that a purchase order can have

45
00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:20.039
one to many items.

46
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.243
Now you have all the necessary elements

47
00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:27.247
to define our Discovery Tool. In order to do this,

48
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:29.249
let's first load the index

49
00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:31.251
on the entity set embeddings

50
00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:32.952
that you created in the last lesson.

51
00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:35.722
Let's also import the helper function

52
00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:39.359
to discover_apis_and_process defined earlier.

53
00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:42.795
Recall that discover_apis_and_process

54
00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:45.765
returns a list of discovered entity sets

55
00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:48.334
along with their business process information.

56
00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.104
Let's try this out with an example query.

57
00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:54.707
Here, I'm looking for APIs and process information

58
00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.477
for the query, show me active purchase orders.

59
00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:00.980
And in the list, you can see that we have

60
00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:05.084
the matching list of entity sets which include PurchaseOrder.

61
00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.420
PurchaseOrder item along with purchase requisition

62
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.689
and purchase requisition item.

63
00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:12.392
Besides which we also retrieve the process information

64
00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:15.595
that PurchaseOrder depends on Purchase Requisition.

65
00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.197
That looks good.

66
00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:21.601
You now have all the key elements required for the tool discovery.

67
00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:25.605
Now you're ready to define this as a tool to the agent

68
00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:29.209
which we can use later. Here we use the tool decorator

69
00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.011
to convert our function discover_apis

70
00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.113
into the discovery tool.

71
00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.016
This function or the tool

72
00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.018
takes a natural language input from the user,

73
00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:42.021
identifies the relevant entity sets

74
00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:46.025
and returns their API specs, including the business process information.

75
00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.695
Now that we have our discovery tool,

76
00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:52.498
let's prepare for API execution with mocked services.

77
00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:56.302
We import helper functions that get,

78
00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:59.272
post, and display our mock data services.

79
00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:02.675
Let's take a look at the content of our mock database.

80
00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:04.978
First, purchase requisitions.

81
00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:07.780
Here you will see that there are two purchase requisitions.

82
00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.283
one ending with number one

83
00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:12.085
and the other ending with two.

84
00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:15.488
While the first purchase requisition has a single item for ruler,

85
00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.490
the second one has two items,

86
00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.093
items 10 and 20 for pencil and pen.

87
00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.664
Now let's look at how a purchase orders dataset looks like.

88
00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.966
Here we have three purchase orders,

89
00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:29.669
those ending with one, two, and three.

90
00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:33.673
and each of them correspond to unique items, ruler, pencil,

91
00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.276
and pen for certain quantities.

92
00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.945
And the thing to note here is that

93
00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.481
each of the item in the purchase order refers

94
00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:44.484
to a corresponding item in the purchase requisition.

95
00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:47.887
So in this case, purchase order one item 10

96
00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:52.892
refers to purchase requisition one item 10, which we saw above.

97
00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.495
And in a similar way, we have purchase order two

98
00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.865
item 10 refers to purchase requisition two

99
00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:02.468
item 10, as in the case above for pencil.

100
00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:05.672
Let's try a mock service for post data.

101
00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:09.075
You see that for the service related to purchase order

102
00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.878
and the entity set related to purchase order,

103
00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:15.381
this particular function makes a single call

104
00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.686
to purchase order API along with the materials that are requested

105
00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:23.056
at the item level. and executes this call

106
00:05:23.256 --> 00:05:25.858
and returns the newly created purchase order,

107
00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:29.062
which is in this case the purchase order ending with four.

108
00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:31.431
In comparison to the previous results,

109
00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:33.433
we now have a new purchase order

110
00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.435
along with two new items

111
00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:39.038
for mouse and keyboard that were requested in the call.

112
00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:43.109
Next, let us call get_data_mock function to retrieve

113
00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:45.611
our first purchase order that matches

114
00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:49.315
the criteria PurchasingGroup 005

115
00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.018
and PurchasingOrganization 3000.

116
00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:54.454
As you can see,

117
00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:57.256
this function displays the OData call

118
00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.459
to the service matching the filter criteria

119
00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:01.961
of interest and returns

120
00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.430
the matching PurchaseOrder.

121
00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.933
In this case, the first PurchaseOrder

122
00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:10.937
with PurchasingGroup and PurchasingOrganization as requested.

123
00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:13.940
Now that you know what these two mock functions do,

124
00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.309
let's define tools for the agent.

125
00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:19.312
Here again we use the tool decorator

126
00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:22.515
to wrap our mock functions for get_api

127
00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:24.617
and post_data_api.

128
00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.153
and convert them into tools.

129
00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:30.156
We now have all the necessary building blocks.

130
00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.358
Let's proceed to create the agent.

131
00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.360
And to create the agent, we'll

132
00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.462
use a predefined agent loop from LangChain.

133
00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:38.798
And now all you need to do

134
00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:41.067
is to provide tools that you previously defined

135
00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.703
along with the prompt. In the prompt,

136
00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.139
we tell the agent to discover APIs

137
00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:48.341
and business process information

138
00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:50.343
based on the user query,

139
00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:54.347
and then call the APIs according to the business process.

140
00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:57.950
We also have to tell the agent how to use navigations

141
00:06:58.451 --> 00:07:00.920
to create header and item level objects

142
00:07:01.254 --> 00:07:05.358
in a single call. Let's now bring everything together.

143
00:07:05.725 --> 00:07:09.162
We provide the tools we defined earlier

144
00:07:09.162 --> 00:07:10.730
along with the prompt we just prepared.

145
00:07:10.797 --> 00:07:13.199
Let's now try this with some example queries.

146
00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:17.303
The first query is related to fetching active purchase orders.

147
00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:22.575
As you can see, the agent first calls discover_apis with the query,

148
00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:25.778
fetches a list of matching APIs,

149
00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:30.583
entity sets, along with their properties, navigation links,

150
00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:33.786
and then also finalizes the process information.

151
00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:39.225
It then calls get_api tool with the service_name related to PURCHASEORDER

152
00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:43.529
and passes the filter_string as it is in the user query.

153
00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:46.732
In this particular case, it is looking for purchase orders

154
00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:48.935
in the ProcessingStatus active.

155
00:07:49.235 --> 00:07:52.238
And then says that there are no active purchase orders

156
00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:56.242
in this particular purchasing group and purchasing organization.

157
00:07:56.576 --> 00:08:00.480
So here the model used the literal value active

158
00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.149
instead of a corresponding technical code

159
00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:05.017
and that is why it is

160
00:08:05.017 --> 00:08:06.786
not able to fetch active purchase orders.

161
00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:11.657
Now we could flexibly add what we call as fixed value helpers

162
00:08:11.958 --> 00:08:16.062
that maps the status code to the status description.

163
00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:19.665
Let's now add this to the knowledge graph.

164
00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:23.703
Here, we add a bunch of related status codes,

165
00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:26.706
for example 01 meaning In process,

166
00:08:26.706 --> 00:08:29.208
02 meaning Active and so on,

167
00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:32.011
to our purchase order entity set

168
00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:34.447
and add it finally to the graph.

169
00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.749
And now, let's try to run

170
00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:39.051
the same query again with the agent.

171
00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:43.556
Now in this case, the agent then actually maps

172
00:08:43.556 --> 00:08:46.259
the PurchasingProcessingStatus to 02

173
00:08:46.259 --> 00:08:49.762
instead of active as it did in the past.

174
00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:53.266
and is now able to correctly find two matching purchase orders

175
00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:55.268
in active status.

176
00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:58.204
So this is a flexibility that knowledge graphs offer

177
00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.806
that it can dynamically extend the graph

178
00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:03.409
with corresponding business entries

179
00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:06.412
and also map them to the agent use cases.

180
00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:09.215
Now, let's take a look at another example.

181
00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:14.220
This time, a bit more complex one related to creating purchase order.

182
00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.689
Here, the agent has to determine that

183
00:09:16.689 --> 00:09:19.225
a purchase requisition has to be created first

184
00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:21.827
and then a purchase order to

185
00:09:21.827 --> 00:09:24.497
be created, which refers the purchase requisition.

186
00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:28.200
As you can see, the agent first does the discovery,

187
00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:31.504
fetches the right APIs along with their specifications

188
00:09:31.504 --> 00:09:33.906
in terms of properties, navigations,

189
00:09:33.906 --> 00:09:35.708
and business process information.

190
00:09:35.708 --> 00:09:37.443
In this time, it recognizes that

191
00:09:37.443 --> 00:09:39.512
it should use the business process information

192
00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:44.483
that states that purchase requisition should be first created

193
00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:46.485
before creating a purchase order.

194
00:09:46.852 --> 00:09:50.356
And so it invokes the post data tool

195
00:09:50.356 --> 00:09:53.659
with the name of the service being purchase requisition.

196
00:09:53.659 --> 00:09:57.363
In a single payload creates the purchase requisition first

197
00:09:57.363 --> 00:10:01.667
and uses that as a reference to call post_data_api

198
00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:04.870
with purchase order API.

199
00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:09.008
And, it creates a new purchase order ending with five,

200
00:10:09.008 --> 00:10:13.312
referring the newly created purchase requisition ending with three.

201
00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:16.082
Let's take a look at our mock database

202
00:10:16.082 --> 00:10:18.384
to see how this changed after the last call.

203
00:10:18.784 --> 00:10:21.687
Here you can see that there is a new purchase requisition

204
00:10:21.687 --> 00:10:23.289
ending with three, item 10.

205
00:10:23.789 --> 00:10:28.294
And now let's look at our Purchase Order mock database.

206
00:10:28.294 --> 00:10:31.998
And there is a new Purchase Order here ending with five.

207
00:10:32.498 --> 00:10:36.602
And importantly, it refers to the Purchase Requisition ending with three

208
00:10:37.003 --> 00:10:38.804
that was just created.

209
00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:42.708
Basically demonstrating that the agent adhered

210
00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:45.311
to the business process rule that a Purchase Requisition

211
00:10:45.311 --> 00:10:50.311
should be first created before creating a Purchase Order.

212
00:10:50.683 --> 00:10:52.985
Now, let's summarize the agent's flow

213
00:10:53.285 --> 00:10:56.255
with our last example related to create purchase order.

214
00:10:56.956 --> 00:11:00.259
It starts with the user input being passed to the agent.

215
00:11:00.726 --> 00:11:03.529
which then decides to call the discovery tool

216
00:11:03.729 --> 00:11:06.732
to find APIs and process information

217
00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:09.535
relevant for the given user input.

218
00:11:09.735 --> 00:11:12.538
The discovery tool in turn calls the vector database,

219
00:11:12.638 --> 00:11:15.341
in our case just a local in-memory index,

220
00:11:15.441 --> 00:11:17.643
to retrieve relevant entity sets.

221
00:11:18.077 --> 00:11:21.781
Next, the tool queries the knowledge graph for business process information

222
00:11:22.181 --> 00:11:25.785
and additional relevant entity sets as well as their metadata.

223
00:11:26.185 --> 00:11:28.487
All of these are then returned to the agent.

224
00:11:29.088 --> 00:11:32.191
Then, the agent has all the relevant information to decide

225
00:11:32.491 --> 00:11:34.794
that it first needs to make a post API call

226
00:11:34.894 --> 00:11:36.796
to create a purchase requisition.

227
00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:40.633
which then calls the corresponding post API tool,

228
00:11:40.733 --> 00:11:42.768
executes the API and returns the

229
00:11:42.768 --> 00:11:45.137
newly created purchase requisition to the agent.

230
00:11:45.638 --> 00:11:48.941
Finally, the agent executes a post API again

231
00:11:49.141 --> 00:11:51.444
to create a purchase order with

232
00:11:51.444 --> 00:11:54.146
reference to the newly created purchase requisition

233
00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:56.415
and returns this back to the agent.
